configure: the null backend may have OpenGL when it brings its own.

ScummVM's configure gives OpenGL only to backends it knows to have one.
The Chimera core is built on the null backend and links its own OpenGL
(Mesa's softpipe, inside the sandbox), so with --opengl-mode=gl the null
backend gets desktop OpenGL through GLAD, which loads every function
through OSystem::getOpenGLProcAddress. Without that option nothing
changes: the null backend has no OpenGL, as upstream.

diff --git a/configure b/configure
index 1f24e389..1a2101f6 100755
--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -6868,6 +6868,17 @@ if test "$_opengl_mode" != none ; then
 			_opengl_mode=gles2
 			_opengl_glad=yes
 			;;
+		null)
+			# A program built on the null backend that brings its own
+			# desktop OpenGL (the Chimera core links Mesa) asks for it with
+			# --opengl-mode=gl; GLAD then loads it through
+			# OSystem::getOpenGLProcAddress. Otherwise there is none.
+			if test "$_opengl_mode" = "gl" ; then
+				_opengl_glad=yes
+			else
+				_opengl_mode=none
+			fi
+			;;
 		*)
 			# On all other platforms, by default don't enable OpenGL
 			test "$_opengl_mode" = "auto" && _opengl_mode=none
